= XWiimote Wayland Plan =

Status: active migration plan for distributions where Wayland is the primary
session and Xorg input drivers are legacy compatibility only.

== Decision ==

The default Wayland path is a Linux uinput bridge, shipped as xwiiwaylandd.
It consumes hid-wiimote/libxwiimote events and creates virtual evdev devices.
Wayland compositors already ingest those devices through libinput and native
game stacks already ingest them through evdev, SDL, Wine, and Proton.

This is intentionally not an XWayland or Xorg input-driver port. Wayland clients
cannot generally inject input into other clients. Compositor-specific virtual
input protocols are not portable enough for the default backend. libei is the
preferred future compositor-mediated backend once it is broadly available; it
will be an optional backend, not a replacement for the uinput baseline.

== Current shipped behavior ==

xwiiwaylandd:
  - monitors hid-wiimote devices through libxwiimote;
  - creates one virtual uinput gamepad per Wii Remote;
  - forwards core, Nunchuk, Classic Controller, Pro Controller, Guitar, and
    Drums button events;
  - forwards Nunchuk, Classic Controller, Pro Controller, and Guitar axes;
  - handles hotplug and extension changes without Xorg configuration.

Required runtime permissions:
  - normal input read access for hid-wiimote evdev nodes;
  - write access to /dev/uinput, normally via a distribution udev rule or a
    system service running with the input/uinput capability policy.

== Roadmap ==

1. Desktop profile
   Add an optional pointer/keyboard profile for Wii Remote core and IR events:
   A/B as mouse buttons, D-pad or IR as pointer movement, +/-/Home as desktop
   keys. Keep the default profile gamepad-safe so games do not see synthetic
   keyboard/mouse events unless requested.

2. Per-device policy
   Add a small configuration file for profiles and axis normalization. Avoid a
   daemon-specific language; use key=value or TOML only if a parser is already
   needed for another feature.

3. Optional libei backend
   Add --backend=libei when libei and compositor support are common enough.
   libei gives user-mediated emulated input through a compositor EIS endpoint;
   uinput remains the fallback because it works across compositors and native
   game stacks today.

4. Distribution integration
   Ship the example systemd user service and udev rule from res/. Keep the
   legacy Xorg blacklist file for Xorg sessions only.

5. Test hardware matrix
   Verify with original Wii Remote, Wii Remote Plus, Nunchuk, Classic
   Controller, Wii U Pro Controller, Guitar, Drums, and Balance Board. Hardware
   reports should record kernel version, compositor, and SDL/Wine behavior.
